Hi @lgrammel, I understand that there are some additional complications with tool calling + streaming, but let me give a simpler example with generateObject.

Here is a simple text classification example. Sometimes, the provider will return a value that falls outside of the specified enum (we see this happen occasionally even when using structured outputs). Let's say that if the LLM returns anything outside of the enum, we just want to replace whatever it gave us with 'neutral'. 

```
async function classifyText(text: string) {
  try {
    return await generateObject({
      model: openai('gpt-4-turbo'),
      schema: z.object({
        category: z.enum(['exciting', 'boring', 'neutral']),
        reasoning: z.string(),
      }),
      prompt: `Classify the following text into one of the following categories: exciting, boring, or neutral: ${text}`,
    });
  } catch (error) {
    if (TypeValidationError.isInstance(error)) {
      const generatedValue = error.value;
      generatedValue.category = 'neutral';
      
      // At this point, we want to treat this as a successful response and return type GenerateObjectResult, but all we have from the TypeValidationError is .value. The rest of the data from the response is completely missing.
      
      return generatedValue;
    }
    throw error;
  }
}

async function main(requestId: string) {
  const text = getText();
  const aiSdkResult = await classifyText(text);
  await customAIRequestLogger({
    function: 'text-classifier',
    requestId,
    input: text,
    output: aiSdkResult.object,
    usage: aiSdkResult.usage,
    // assume we are logging other data, such as `system_fingerprint` or `cached_tokens` 
  });
  await saveResult({
    requestId,
    result: aiSdkResult.object,
  });
}
```

Right now, this will throw a `AI_TypeValidationError`, which only has `value` and `message`, even though the inference provider sent back a 200, with all of the other metadata/usage information we would expect if we called the provider directly. 

The core problem that I'm trying to point out is that by using the Vercel AI SDK, we can no longer access that 200 response because the SDK is throwing a type validation error.

In the example above, if we get a AI_TypeValidationError, we can repair it and want to be able to treat this as a successful response. We _could_ try to reconstruct a successful `GenerateObjectResult` in the `catch` block, but we'd only be able to estimate the usage tokens with our own tokenizer and pass the result back, and we'd be missing other fields on the response. 

At the end of the day, the AI SDK is an abstraction on top of making requests to inference providers, so I'd expect to be able to access all of the data returned from those requests, especially the successful ones.

I hope this helps clarify the issue I'm trying to point out, and that it doesn't come off as complaining! I love the AI SDK and all of the work you are doing on it, and I'm just trying to give feedback to help make it better. I understand getting the full response from the provider may not be possible in tool calling / streaming situations, but I do think there should be a better solution here.

