@lgrammel here's what I got from o4 (I started digging when I saw the SWR usage):

_It turns out the culprit is that we were leaning on SWRV to hold our “status” state, but because we never actually re-fetched it from a real endpoint SWRV’s internals don’t always fire a proper reactive update—especially if you switch tabs or lose focus while you’re mid-stream. The simplest fix is: ditch SWRV for status and just use a plain Vue ref. That way you have full control over when and how it updates._

```ts
  // ↓ instead, use a plain ref…
  const status = ref<'submitted' | 'streaming' | 'ready' | 'error'>('ready');

  // Wrap a “mutate” that always resolves – so the rest of the code can stay the same:
  const mutateStatus = (
    updater: ('submitted' | 'streaming' | 'ready' | 'error')
           | ((current: typeof status.value) => typeof status.value)
  ): Promise<typeof status.value> => {
    status.value =
      typeof updater === 'function'
        ? (updater as (s: typeof status.value) => typeof status.value)(status.value)
        : updater;
    return Promise.resolve(status.value);
  };
