I think the underlying issue is that [removing incomplete tool calls](https://github.com/vercel/ai/blob/60f2db8f6abc04e8401e67f41047e94f8110584b/packages/ai/src/ui/convert-to-model-messages.ts#L39-L49) breaks reasoning, even though that's the recommended approach for incomplete tool calls (see https://github.com/vercel/ai/issues/7097). I wonder if completing incomplete tool calls would be a better approach? That works for me:

```js
  return messages.map(message => ({
    ...message,
    parts: message.parts?.map(part => {
      if ('state' in part && part.state === 'input-available') {
        return {
          ...part,
          state: 'output-available',
          output: 'Completed',
        };
      }
      return part;
    })
  }));
```

This middleware seems to effectively create the behavior that I'm expecting:

```ts
const systemPromptToInstructionsMiddleware: LanguageModelMiddleware = {
  async transformParams(options) {
    const prompt = options.params.prompt;
    if (prompt[0].role === "system") {
      options.params.providerOptions = {
        ...(options.params.providerOptions ?? {}),
        openai: {
          ...(options.params.providerOptions?.openai ?? {}),
          instructions: prompt[0].content,
        },
      };
      prompt.shift();
    }
    return options.params;
  },
};

const responsesModel = wrapLanguageModel({
  model: client.responses('some-model'),
  middleware: [systemPromptToInstructionsMiddleware],
})
```

this should solve the problem for my team's use case but it feels a bit of a janky solution.

> Can you explain why it makes a difference in your case to send the system message as instructions vs as system input message?

this came to my attention b/c my team has developed our own version of the Responses API. It's the same spec as OpenAI version with some customizations. [Full docs here](https://mongodb.github.io/chatbot/server/responses-api). We currently handle the `instructions` parameters differently from system messages passed to the input.

> a) afaik openai maps system/developer parts correctly behind the scenes in a way that is equivalent to using instruction

