Maybe we could take this opportunity to discuss what should go in the `input` and `output` of a tool-call result for provider executed tool calls like web search.

Currently, for Anthropic, it's quite straightforward - input is an object with property query representing the web search query

```ts
{ query: string }
```

And, success result is an array of sources that were visited as part of the web search call.

https://github.com/vercel/ai/blob/ec64c2793f94d00a229ee3e70aec3d497a22a670/packages/anthropic/src/anthropic-messages-language-model.ts#L891-L904

Similarly, error result is an object that includes the error reason

https://github.com/vercel/ai/blob/ec64c2793f94d00a229ee3e70aec3d497a22a670/packages/anthropic/src/anthropic-messages-language-model.ts#L921-L931

It is not so formulated for OpenAI web search: the input field of this tool-result chunk is set to the action returned in the tool call result and output is an object containing the status of the tool call. This action object is a discriminated union and can either be a search action or page open action or find action, depending on the model (more in the OpenAI's [docs](https://platform.openai.com/docs/guides/tools-web-search?api-mode=responses#output-and-citations) and OpenAI's [node repository](https://github.com/openai/openai-node/blob/c8ee824c536ec36d2c554299275f9694040de67c/src/resources/responses/responses.ts#L2032-L2083)).

```ts
{
    type: "search";
    query?: string;
    sources?: Array<{ type: "url", url: string }>;
} | {
    type: "open_page";
    url: string;
} | {
    type: "find";
    url: string;
    pattern: string;
}
```

It seems to me that if we want to be consistent with input being the search query used and output being the sources that were visited, we should check the type of the action and prepare input and output accordingly.

An even nicer way of handling `yield`ing tool invocations would likely be to:

- having a separate property that aggregates the `yield`s in an array, and
- having a separate property that contains the `return`ed value

It'd be nice to have all yielded results available and let me pick myself in the UI if I only care about the `.index(-1)` one

## Example

```ts
tool({
