I get duplicate messages and sources, using the latest beta ai sdk and @ai-sdk/xai. I fix it with the following helpers to hack:

```ts
function deduped_paragraph(text: string): string {
  if (text.length === 0) {
    return ''
  }

  // Check if the text is split in half and both halves are the same
  const half_length = Math.floor(text.length / 2)
  const first_half = text.slice(0, half_length)
  const second_half = text.slice(half_length)
  if (first_half === second_half) {
    return first_half
  }
  return text
}

function remove_portions_from_previous_paragraphs(existing_paragraphs: string[], new_text: string): string {
  let cleaned_text = new_text

  for (const existing_paragraph of existing_paragraphs) {
    let index = cleaned_text.indexOf(existing_paragraph)
    while (index !== -1) {
      cleaned_text = cleaned_text.slice(0, index) + cleaned_text.slice(index + existing_paragraph.length)
      index = cleaned_text.indexOf(existing_paragraph, index)
    }
  }

  return cleaned_text.trim()
}

function is_duplicate_paragraph(existing_paragraphs: string[], new_text: string): boolean {
  return existing_paragraphs.includes(new_text)
}
```

Enable multi-agent conversations without awkward hacks. Below I’ll explain the pros/cons of a couple approaches, show a recommended shape (backwards-compatible), and give small TypeScript + React examples you can drop into a UI to support multiple agents in a single `UIMessageStream`.

# Two possible designs

### Option A — Extend `role` to encode agent ids (quick, but messy)

Example:
`role: "triage-agent"` or `role: "agent:triage-agent"`

**Pros**

* Minimal schema change (no new field).

**Cons**

* Conflates role semantics (role is normally `user|assistant|system|tool`) and makes logic that branches on `role` brittle.
* Harder to maintain or validate.
* Less explicit for metadata (display name, avatar, etc).

### Option B — Preferred: add an explicit `agentId` (backwards compatible)

Keep `role` semantics intact and add an optional `agentId` field. If `agentId` is present, the message is attributed to that agent. If absent, fall back to the existing `role` handling.

**Pros**

* Explicit and semantic.
* Backwards compatible (old UIs ignore the new field).
* Easier to attach metadata (name/avatar/policies) via an agent registry.
* Safer for validation and display logic.

# Recommended schema (JSON / TypeScript)

```ts
// message parts remain the same
type UIMessagePart = { type: 'text' | 'image' | 'json' ; text?: string; /* ... */ };

type Role = 'user' | 'assistant' | 'system' | 'tool';

// core message
interface UIMessage {
  id: string;
  role: Role;
  agentId?: string; // NEW: optional agent identifier, e.g. "triage-agent"
  parts: UIMessagePart[];
  createdAt?: string;
  // optional: provenance metadata (server-only)
  source?: { origin: 'server' | 'client'; actorId?: string };
}
```

# Example conversation (your example)

```json
[
  {
    "id": "m1",
    "role": "user",
    "parts": [{ "type": "text", "text": "My wifi is not working" }]
  },
  {
    "id": "m2",
    "role": "assistant",
    "agentId": "triage-agent",
    "parts": [{ "type": "text", "text": "I will connect you with technical support" }]
  },
  {
    "id": "m3",
    "role": "assistant",
    "agentId": "tech-support-agent",
    "parts": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Please restart your device" }]
  }
]
```

Note: keep `role: "assistant"` for agent responses (preserves any existing logic that treats `assistant` specially), but attach `agentId` to identify which assistant/agent produced it. You could also allow `role: "agent"` if you prefer, but I recommend keeping `assistant` for compatibility.

# Agent registry / metadata

On the client (or server), maintain an `agents` map:

```ts
type AgentInfo = {
  id: string;
  displayName: string;
  avatarUrl?: string;
  description?: string;
};

const agents: Record<string, AgentInfo> = {
  'triage-agent': { id: 'triage-agent', displayName: 'Triage', avatarUrl: '/avatars/triage.png' },
  'tech-support-agent': { id: 'tech-support-agent', displayName: 'Tech Support', avatarUrl: '/avatars/tech.png' },
};
```

# Simple render example (React)

```tsx
function getAuthorForMessage(msg: UIMessage, agents: Record<string, AgentInfo>) {
  if (msg.agentId && agents[msg.agentId]) return agents[msg.agentId];
  switch (msg.role) {
    case 'user': return { displayName: 'You' };
    case 'assistant': return { displayName: 'Assistant' }; // fallback assistant
    case 'system': return { displayName: 'System' };
    default: return { displayName: 'Unknown' };
  }
}

function Message({ msg, agents }: { msg: UIMessage; agents: Record<string, AgentInfo> }) {
  const author = getAuthorForMessage(msg, agents);
  return (
    <div className="message">
      <img className="avatar" src={author.avatarUrl ?? '/avatars/default.png'} />
      <div className="meta">
        <strong>{author.displayName}</strong>
        <div className="text">{msg.parts.map(p => p.type === 'text' ? p.text : null)}</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}
```

# Grouping & stream behavior

* Group sequential messages that share the same `(agentId || role)` so UI looks natural for multi-message responses from the same agent.
* Keep original `createdAt` timestamps so ordering across agents is preserved.

# Security & server-side validation

* Do **not** let the client set `agentId` arbitrarily when sending messages — validate server-side, otherwise users can spoof agent identities.
* Only the server (or an authorized orchestration layer) should issue messages on behalf of agents.
* If messages originate from actual Agent processes, include provenance metadata (`source`, `actorId`, signature, etc.) to allow the UI to trust the attribution.

# Moderation, analytics, and tooling

* Preserve `role` for moderation or analytics pipelines that rely on `assistant` vs `user`.
* Add `agentId` to logs/events so you can analyze agent performance independently.

# Migration path

1. Add `agentId` as an optional field in the message model.
2. Update UI renderers to use `agentId` when present (fallback to `role`).
3. Update server code that issues assistant messages to attach `agentId`.
4. Optionally add a `middleware/transform` step that converts older messages if needed.

