> We're having a similar issue as well, if you abort an ongoing stream, the state of the last text step will be 'streaming', and we get an error that: 'Item with id: 'rs_...' not found", even though the reasoning step is there, and any following assistant messages on following user messages will be empty.
> 
> We're not sure what the best way to handle this is.

## The Problem

After some investigation, it seems there are multiple issues. Firstly, most models dislike incomplete tool calls. Fortunately, there is a flag with `convertToModelMessages` called `ignoreIncompleteToolCalls`, which will just remove those tool calls. This works for most cases.

However, if using reasoning with an OpenAI model, interruptions cause havoc, as discussed in https://github.com/vercel/ai/issues/8811 and https://github.com/coder/blink/issues/34

In cases where:
- reasoning is interrupted (reasoning state = streaming)
- text output following a reasoning (text state = streaming)
- tool call following reasoning is removed (which is what ignoreIncompleteToolCalls does)

Errors can also happen when a non-reasoning OpenAI model (e.g. GPT-4o) are interrupted.

Then OpenAI will always throw errors if you try to follow it up with another message.

## The Solution

OpenAI does validating using the metadata. Each OpenAI message has an ItemId, and possibly reasoning data. E.g:

```json
{
  "id": "8AoTbXaZ16HVM98",
  "role": "assistant",
  "parts": [
    {
      "type": "step-start"
    },
    {
      "type": "reasoning",
      "text": "",
      "providerMetadata": {
        "openai": {
          "itemId": "rs_XXXXXXXXXX",
          "reasoningEncryptedContent": null
        }
      },
      "state": "streaming"
    }
  ]
}
```

OpenAI will only reject interrupted messages if it can identify it using the itemId. Messages without this metadata don't trigger the error. You can test this by starting a conversation with Claude or Gemini (which don't produce such identifiers), and then switching to an OpenAI model, and even interrupted messages are accepted into the context window (I assume because requiring it wouldn't be backwards compatible and very inflexible).

So the solution is to just remove the `itemId` from the metadata whenever we can determine that that message would cause an error on their side. I have implemented my own `convertToModelMessages` function, which takes a second flag, `ignoreIncompleteReasoning`. This function basically wraps the built-in `convertToModelMessages` function, but applies the `itemId` stripping. Fully type safe too.

You can find it here: https://gist.github.com/SamStenner/336806b35c19743c354b8e20757d6d2d

Just use it like this:
```typescript
messages: convertToModelMessages(messages, {
  ignoreIncompleteToolCalls: true,
  ignoreIncompleteReasoning: true,
})
```

Seems to work well for us!

Raw code again here:
```typescript
import {
  convertToModelMessages,
  isToolUIPart,
  ModelMessage,
  UIMessage as BaseUIMessage,
} from "ai";

type ConvertParameters<UIMessage extends BaseUIMessage> = Parameters<
  typeof convertToModelMessages<UIMessage>
>;

type Options<UIMessage extends BaseUIMessage> =
  ConvertParameters<UIMessage>[1] & {
    ignoreIncompleteReasoning?: boolean;
  };

type MessagePart = BaseUIMessage["parts"][number];

type StreamablePart = Extract<MessagePart, { type: "reasoning" | "text" }>;

function hasProviderMetadata(
  part: MessagePart,
): part is StreamablePart & {
  providerMetadata: NonNullable<StreamablePart["providerMetadata"]>;
} {
  return (
    (part.type === "reasoning" || part.type === "text") &&
    "providerMetadata" in part &&
    part.providerMetadata !== undefined
  );
}

function removeItemId<T extends MessagePart>(part: T): T {
  if (!hasProviderMetadata(part)) return part;
  return {
    ...part,
    providerMetadata: {
      ...part.providerMetadata,
      openai: {
        ...part.providerMetadata?.openai,
        itemId: undefined,
      },
    },
  } as T;
}

function isPartIncomplete(part: MessagePart): boolean {
  if (
    (part.type === "text" || part.type === "reasoning") &&
    part.state === "streaming"
  ) {
    return true;
  }
  return (
    (part.type === "dynamic-tool" || isToolUIPart(part)) &&
    (part.state === "input-streaming" || part.state === "input-available")
  );
}

function findNextNonReasoningPart(
  parts: MessagePart[],
  startIndex: number,
): MessagePart | undefined {
  for (let i = startIndex + 1; i < parts.length; i++) {
    const part = parts[i];
    if (part.type !== "reasoning") return part;
  }
  return undefined;
}

function shouldRemoveItemId(
  part: MessagePart,
  parts: MessagePart[],
  index: number,
): boolean {
  if (!hasProviderMetadata(part)) return false;
  if (!part.providerMetadata?.openai?.itemId) return false;
  if (part.state === "streaming") return true;
  if (part.type === "reasoning" && part.state === "done") {
    const nextPart = findNextNonReasoningPart(parts, index);
    if (nextPart && isPartIncomplete(nextPart)) {
      return true;
    }
  }
  return false;
}

function convertToOpenAICompatibleModelMessages<
  UIMessage extends BaseUIMessage,
>(
  messages: ConvertParameters<UIMessage>[0],
  options?: Options<UIMessage>,
): ModelMessage[] {
  const { ignoreIncompleteReasoning, ...rest } = options ?? {};
  if (!ignoreIncompleteReasoning) return convertToModelMessages(messages, rest);
  const modified = messages.map((message) => {
    if (message.role !== "assistant") return message;
    const streamableParts = message.parts.filter(
      (part) => part.type === "reasoning" || part.type === "text",
    );
    if (!streamableParts.length) return message;
    return {
      ...message,
      parts: message.parts.map((part, index) => {
        return shouldRemoveItemId(part, message.parts, index)
          ? removeItemId(part)
          : part;
      }),
    };
  });
  return convertToModelMessages(modified, rest);
}

export { convertToOpenAICompatibleModelMessages as convertToModelMessages };
```

