This has two issues: First, Gemini actually does support `text/markdown`. Second, because this is Gemini, it should actually not download the file locally, but let Gemini handle the download instead.

If I do the same with the `gateway` provider, it accepts the attachment without an issue and sends me a valid completions response. The image is also not downloaded locally, although I can't verify whether it is actually passed as a URL to Gemini or downloaded on the AI Gateway. I would suggest a similar behavior for the `openAICompatible` provider. 

In regard to type safety, we could design it in the following way:

```
const vertexOpenAI = createOpenAICompatible({
  name: "vertex",
  baseURL: `...`,
  queryParams: { ... },
  allowedMediaTypes: ["text/markdown", ... ],
  supportedUrls: { "image/*": "/*/" } // Identical syntax to writing a custom provider
});
```

While this could provide theoretical type-safety, in practice I see the benefit as low, because I'd argue that most mediaTypes are runtime derived.

Looking at the situation today:
1. The `mediaType` attribute on the `FilePart` is actually not a typed attribute (no matter which provider I use)
2. The `openAICompatibleProvider` accepts `mediaType: "image/*"` already, which is a partial wildcard

That's why I'm suggesting not to add `allowedMedaTypes` as provider setting, but let the downstream service handle any parameter validation (like the AI Gateway does). On the other hand, `supportedUrls` is a behavior modifier, and would have to be passed manually.

Of course, this is a different situation when you implement your own custom openAICompatible provider. In that case you would want to be able to pass a set of allowedMediaTypes internally to [convert-to-openai-compatible-chat-messages](https://github.com/vercel/ai/blob/main/packages/openai-compatible/src/chat/convert-to-openai-compatible-chat-messages.ts#L45) (similar like you already pass supportedUrls).

