We've shipped an external package that takes a different angle on this: behavioral telemetry rather than receipts.

**[@agentlair/vercel-ai](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@agentlair/vercel-ai)** hooks into `experimental_telemetry` and forwards tool calls, completions, errors, and token counts to [AgentLair](https://agentlair.dev)'s behavioral event API. The events feed a trust score algorithm — agents build verifiable track records over time.

```ts
import { createAgentLairExporter } from '@agentlair/vercel-ai';

const agentlair = createAgentLairExporter({
  apiKey: process.env.AGENTLAIR_API_KEY!,
  agentId: 'my-agent',
});

const result = await generateText({
  model: openai('gpt-4o'),
  prompt: 'Hello',
  experimental_telemetry: {
    isEnabled: true,
    tracer: agentlair,
  },
});
```

Zero deps, works with any model provider. The trust profile is public and embeddable — useful for multi-agent systems where one agent needs to evaluate another before delegating work.

Different from the AAR proposal here (which focuses on per-action cryptographic signatures), but solves a related problem: "should I trust this agent's output?"

For LangChain.js / LangGraph users: [@agentlair/langchain](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@agentlair/langchain) ships the same behavioral telemetry via a callback handler.

```ts
import { AgentLairCallbackHandler, injectAat } from '@agentlair/langchain';

const handler = new AgentLairCallbackHandler({ apiKey: process.env.AGENTLAIR_API_KEY, agentId: 'my-agent' });
await agent.invoke({ messages }, injectAat(aat.token, { callbacks: [handler] }));
```

Captures tool_start/end/error, llm_start/end, and agent_action events. Same trust profile as @agentlair/vercel-ai. Mixed stacks (some chains in LangGraph, some in Vercel AI SDK) write to the same agent identity.

Zero hard deps. `@langchain/core` is an optional peer.

