On @rpelevin's signing/anchoring split: one implementation data point in favor of reserving the join now. We implement exactly that separation in protect-mcp (npm), and it has held up in production use: every receipt carries the canonical content digest and the previous chain head; a claim commits to a Merkle root over the complete record; and anchoring that root to an external log is optional and out-of-band (a heartbeat checkpoint command), so a local verifier checks tamper evidence and fork detection from bytes alone, and non-equivocation across auditors is added only where a deployment needs it. The head really is the continuity identity of the stream: because the receipt shape reserved it from the start, anchoring bolted on later without touching the signing layer or any consumer.

For the SDK-facing schema question, it may also be useful that this receipt layer has a standards-track spec: draft-farley-acta-signed-receipts (rev 02 on the IETF datatracker), with a shared conformance suite (ScopeBlind/agent-governance-testvectors) that independent implementations cross-verify against for byte-identical canonical output, including the negative vectors (tampered field, chain gap, stale policy digest) that make a verifier trustworthy.

@Cyberweasel777 the AAR field set and the draft's envelope are close enough that convergence looks cheap: same primitives, same what/who/when/proof decomposition. Happy to map fields so an AAR receipt and an Acta receipt verify under shared tooling rather than forking the category; the conformance suite is open to any implementation that round-trips the vectors.

@babyblueviper1 agreed on the distinct-state point, and it is now more than an argument: the fixture set that landed this week in the TRACE crosswalk (agentrust-io/trace-spec#98) encodes exactly that principle. Three of the six conformance fixtures are validly signed receipts that each fail exactly one non-signature check, with the expected outcome per check machine-readable in an expected.json rather than asserted in prose: wrong chain position (signature pass, chain recomputation fail), stale policy binding (signature pass, freshness fail), and wrong session (signature pass, binding fail). A verifier that collapses these to one "invalid" state cannot tell an auditor whether the record was forged, reordered, out-of-date, or replayed, and those have very different incident responses.

Your anchoring case is the natural fourth member of that family, and your two-failure-modes framing is the right justification: a compromised signer and a captured or stalled anchor fail differently, so the states have to be nameable separately or the failure diagnosis lies. I will add "signature valid, anchor stale" and "signature valid, anchor missing" as distinct expected verifier states to the shared vector suite (ScopeBlind/agent-governance-testvectors), consistent with anchoring staying an optional out-of-band checkpoint rather than part of the signing step.

For the AAR middleware question in this issue, the takeaway stands independently of any particular format: whatever receipt shape ships, the verifier contract should enumerate its checks and report them separately, because that is what makes negative test vectors possible at all.

