Following up with a concrete TypeScript-native approach for the Vercel AI SDK that avoids the Python subprocess dependency mentioned in the issue.

The **OWASP Agent Memory Guard** core algorithm (https://github.com/OWASP/www-project-agent-memory-guard) is a pattern, not just a Python package. Here's how it maps to the Vercel AI SDK's `streamText` / `generateText` message pipeline:

```typescript
import { streamText, CoreMessage } from 'ai';

// Memory guard middleware for Vercel AI SDK
async function guardedMessages(messages: CoreMessage[]): Promise<CoreMessage[]> {
  const scanned = await Promise.all(
    messages.map(async (msg) => {
      if (msg.role === 'tool' || msg.role === 'user') {
        const content = typeof msg.content === 'string' ? msg.content : JSON.stringify(msg.content);
        const isSafe = await scanForInjection(content); // OWASP AMG pattern
        if (!isSafe) {
          return { ...msg, content: '[BLOCKED: potential memory poisoning attempt]' };
        }
      }
      return msg;
    })
  );
  return scanned;
}

// Usage with streamText
const result = await streamText({
  model: openai('gpt-4o'),
  messages: await guardedMessages(messages), // scan before each LLM call
  tools: { ... },
});
```

The `scanForInjection` function can be implemented as a lightweight TypeScript port of the OWASP AMG heuristics, or as a call to the Python sidecar. For a test corpus of real-world memory poisoning payloads to validate this pattern, **AgentThreatBench** (https://github.com/vgudur-dev/AgentThreatBench) has labeled examples specifically for the `streamText`/`useChat` message history attack surface.

